# fancy-gol — core rules

**`.agents/` is the source of truth for this project.** Read `.agents/AGENTS.md` in full before
doing anything. It is the operating manual; these rules are a summary of it.

| Need | File |
|---|---|
| The contract for agents | `.agents/AGENTS.md` |
| The vision and the standard | `.agents/docs/INCEPTION.md` |
| Current status and the next task | `.agents/dashboard.html` |
| Rules that apply everywhere | `.agents/planning/README.md` §3 |
| Binding architecture decisions | `.agents/planning/ARCHITECTURE_DECISIONS.md` (ADR-001…010) |
| Exactly what to build | `.agents/planning/PHASE_<0-6>_*.md` |

## What this is

A cellular automata simulator that intends to be **fabulous**, built as an open agentic coding
experiment — the agent files are in the repo on purpose. Someone will read your commits.

The bar is **excellent, optimised, and fabulous**, never merely "working":

- **Excellent** — a beginner uses it without documentation; an expert has everything at their fingertips.
- **Optimised** — nothing allocates in a hot loop, nothing re-renders without a state change, every budget is measured and gated.
- **Fabulous** — if it is boring, it is not finished.

## The seven standing rules (from INCEPTION.md)

1. **No Bloat** — if a library can be written in 50 lines of TS, don't import a package.
   The *entire* permitted runtime dependency surface is `express` and `ws`. The ruleset validator,
   RLE codec, charting module, fuzzy matcher, easing solvers, audio synthesis, boundary checker and
   changelog automation are hand-written **by design**, each a named task.
2. **Pure Logic** — the simulation logic must never know the UI exists.
3. **Stay Fancy** — if a feature is boring, find the version of it that is visually interesting.
4. **Automated Proof** — every step passes its Vitest suite before it is "done".
5. **Agit-Prop** — if the code is "just okay", it is not finished. This applies to your own output.
6. **Changelog** — Keep-a-Changelog format and semver from commit one, updated in the same commit
   as the change, never retroactively.
7. **Conventional commits** — small, atomic, logically parsed, each independently green.

## Never

- Add a runtime dependency beyond `express` and `ws` without meeting the No Bloat bar.
- Weaken a coverage threshold, boundary rule, performance budget or visual baseline to make a build pass.
- Re-baseline a failing benchmark or screenshot without investigating and recording why.
- Delete or renumber a task ID — mark it `- [-]` with a reason.
- Present an approximation as exact. Sampled, downsampled and degraded outputs are always labelled.
- Silently discard user data or history. Destructive actions require explicit, informative confirmation.

## Escalate rather than guess

Stop and ask when an ADR looks wrong or two conflict, when acceptance criteria cannot be met as
written, when a budget can only be met by breaking a design principle, or when a gate cannot be
made green. Record the answer in the relevant document — a decision that lives only in a chat log
did not happen.
